Publishing Luvie to the AUR
===========================

The Arch User Repository is not a package format -- it is a collection of git
repositories, each holding a PKGBUILD (a build recipe) and a generated .SRCINFO
(its metadata, in a form the AUR web front end can parse). There is no artifact
for CI to build and attach to a release: `makepkg` turns the PKGBUILD into a
.pkg.tar.zst on the *user's* machine, at install time.

So unlike the .deb and .rpm, this is published by hand, and the file beside this
one is the master copy. It is versioned here rather than only in the AUR repo so
that a change to the build -- a new dependency, a new install path, a moved
CMake option -- can be made in the same commit as the change that caused it.


One-time setup
--------------

1. Make an account at https://aur.archlinux.org and add an SSH public key to it
   (My Account -> SSH Public Key).

2. Point ssh at the right key and user:

       # ~/.ssh/config
       Host aur.archlinux.org
           User aur
           IdentityFile ~/.ssh/aur

3. Clone the (initially empty) package repository. Cloning a name that does not
   exist yet is how a new AUR package is created:

       git clone ssh://aur@aur.archlinux.org/luvie.git aur-luvie


At each release
---------------

Do this after the GitHub release is published, so the tag the PKGBUILD points at
is definitely there.

1. Copy the PKGBUILD across and set the version. pkgver is the tag without its
   leading "v"; pkgrel goes back to 1 for a new version, and is bumped instead
   when the packaging changes but the version does not.

       cp packaging/aur/PKGBUILD ../aur-luvie/
       cd ../aur-luvie
       # edit pkgver / pkgrel

2. Build it. This is also the only real test the PKGBUILD gets, so do not skip
   it -- and prefer the clean-chroot form, which catches a dependency that is
   missing from `depends` but happens to be installed on your machine:

       makepkg -si                     # quick check, uses your installed packages
       extra-x86_64-build              # clean chroot (devtools package)

   Then lint it:

       namcap PKGBUILD
       namcap luvie-*.pkg.tar.zst

3. Regenerate .SRCINFO. It is derived entirely from the PKGBUILD and the AUR
   rejects a push whose .SRCINFO does not match, so this is not optional and
   must be redone every time the PKGBUILD changes:

       makepkg --printsrcinfo > .SRCINFO

4. Commit and push. Only PKGBUILD and .SRCINFO belong in the repository -- not
   the built package, not the sources, not src/ or pkg/:

       git add PKGBUILD .SRCINFO
       git commit -m "Update to 0.0.5"
       git push

5. Copy any edits back to packaging/aur/PKGBUILD in this repository, so the two
   do not drift.


Notes on the recipe
-------------------

It builds from the git tag, not from a release tarball, because the version
string comes from `git describe --tags` (cmake/ProjectVersion.cmake and
cmake/GitVersion.cmake) -- a plain source archive has no git metadata and would
produce a binary reporting 0.0.0.

The build downloads FLTK, LV2, RtMidi, liblo and nlohmann/json at configure
time, so it needs network access during build(), which AUR reviewers do not love
but is unavoidable while FLTK 1.5 remains unreleased and pinned to a commit.

CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 is there because CMake 4 -- which is what Arch
ships -- refuses to configure a project declaring a cmake_minimum_required below
3.5, and nlohmann/json v3.11.3 declares "3.1...3.14". Bumping that pin to
v3.11.4 or later would let the flag go.

The plugin goes to /usr/lib/lv2, which is both the LV2 specification's system
location and where Arch's own LV2 packages put their bundles.
